    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Vector",
    "problem": "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-4,5)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$",
    "worked_solution": "First, notice that the position vector of $P$ is $p=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $n=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is normal to the plane. \n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
